Question: "how do we handle the priority sort and the cursor tiebreaker in a single query."

Design:

1. Sort expression resolution (server-side allowlist only). Map sortBy → a SQL expression built from a literal allowlist in TypeScript; never interpolate the client string:
   - relevance → ts_rank(t.search_vector, q), 400 unless q is present
   - createdAt → t.created_at, updatedAt, dueDate → columns
   - priority → CASE t.priority WHEN 'urgent' THEN 4 WHEN 'high' THEN 3 WHEN 'medium' THEN 2 WHEN 'low' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
     - Note: ELSE 0 handles E12 (unknown stored values don't match a filter — and don't sort garbage: they sort below 'low', deterministic, no 500).
     - Alternative: ORDER BY CASE ... END DESC is fine at board scale; if you wanted an index-friendly sort, a generated priority_rank smallint column would allow a composite btree — but that's speculative; keep the CASE and accept a sort-per-query within the workspace slice. In fact, worth mentioning as a later refinement: a generated STORED column priority_rank + index (project_id, priority_rank, id). But sorting per workspace at limit≤100 with keyset… hmm, keyset doesn't require an index-ordered scan to be O(limit) per page? A btree on the sort key gives the planner a top-N via an index scan; without it: sort the entire match set. At 1M total rows / ~100k per workspace, sorting 100k rows per query ≈ 20-50ms — tolerable but not great; the keyset predicate WHERE (expr, id) < ($v, $id) still scans and filters. Honest position: for priority, add the generated column later; ship with CASE. Actually let me think whether to recommend priority_rank generated column now. Phase 1 says "no schema (model) changes needed — search_vector already exists" and only requires two composite indexes. The ADR's implementation strategy can note: ship v1 with CASE; priority_rank generated column + partial/composite index is the documented escape hatch if p95 shows sort cost. That's a reasonable staged decision. Hmm, but the user asked for a concrete implementation strategy "how to handle priority sort and cursor tiebreaker in a single query" — the answer is: define the sort expression once via a shared SQL fragment and use it in all three of ORDER BY, the keyset predicate (row comparison), and the SELECT (for cursor-encoding v) — so the cursor value and the order always agree by construction. This "single source of truth for sortExpr" is the key implementation insight.
   
2. Row-value comparison keyset with NULLS LAST. FR-3.5/FR-4.4: Postgres row comparison (a, b) < (x, y) is lexicographic and doesn't obey NULLS LAST semantics symmetrically: in a row comparison, NULLs are handled per element with the < semantics: (NULL, 5) < (3, 1) → NULL? Row comparison involving NULL is not simple true/false — ROW(1,NULL) < ROW(1,2) is NULL (indeterminate) → filtered out by WHERE; ROW(1,NULL) < ROW(2,1) → TRUE (compares first element). So a naive row-value predicate with NULLable sort keys (dueDate) is buggy. Two clean fixes:
   - (a) Sentinel coalesce: for desc, COALESCE(sort_expr, '-infinity'::timestamp) (NULL sorts last in desc → NULL must compare as the smallest value), for asc COALESCE(expr, 'infinity'). Since NULLS LAST in both directions means NULL behaves as the extreme of the order, mapping NULL to the order-extreme sentinel makes the row comparison exactly mirror ORDER BY expr DESC NULLS LAST. Dates: ±infinity timestamps; numbers: ±infinity floats, or large ints; strings: '' / max — careful with title VARCHAR: for desc use COALESCE(title,'') (empty string sorts before any title… actually title is NOT NULL in the schema — title String @db.VarChar(200), not nullable! dueDate nullable, estimatedHours nullable but not a sort key in v1… sort keys: relevance (ts_rank, not null), createdAt (not null), updatedAt (not null), dueDate (nullable!), priority CASE (not null — CASE returns 0), title (not null). So dueDate is the only nullable sort key. Clean: COALESCE(t.due_date, '-infinity'::timestamptz) for desc, 'infinity' for asc. And cursor encoding v = ISO string or null.
   - (b) Explicit expansion: (expr < $v) OR (expr = $v AND id < $cid) plus NULL branches — verbose, error-prone; prefer (a). Recommend (a): the sentinel mirrors NULLS LAST into the row comparison, and the same expression is reused in the ORDER BY.
   - Cursor value for a NULL dueDate row: SELECT returns the COALESCE'd expr — so cursor v is the infinity sentinel serialized… ISO for '-infinity' timestamp? Prisma/PG: can't cleanly pass back '-infinity' — actually you can pass the literal string '-infinity'::timestamptz. Simpler: cursor stores v: null for NULL rows, and the predicate binds COALESCE'd sentinel anyway: WHERE (COALESCE(...), id) < (COALESCE($cursorV, '-infinity'::timestamptz), $cursorId). Wait careful: for desc, NULL is last; a page starting after a NULL-due row should return only other NULL rows with smaller id. Cursor v for NULL row under desc = sentinel '-infinity'… then the predicate (coalesce(due,'-inf'), id) < ('-inf', cid) → rows with NULL due (coalesced to -inf) AND id < cid → correct; rows with real dates > -inf excluded → correct. Symmetric for asc with +inf. Good — this works exactly because COALESCE maps NULLS-LAST semantics onto the lexicographic comparison. For relevance (ts_rank) v: rank is a float — cursor encoding uses number. Edge: ranks tie at 0 often (very common with the default ts_rank normalization — many rows share the same rank value; that's why the id tiebreaker is load-bearing).
   - One subtlety: ts_rank recomputed on re-pagination is not stable under concurrent updates (E10 accepted: point-in-time), fine.
   - Another subtlety: id is a uuid. ORDER BY id DESC on uuid is a deterministic ordering by uuid byte order — fine as a tiebreaker (total order); "id ascending == insertion order" is not guaranteed with uuid v4 — irrelevant, only determinism required. Cursor comparison id < $cid compares uuid — Postgres supports uuid comparison (btree on uuid exists). Good.

3. Single-query composition. Build with Prisma.sql:
      const sortExpr = { 
     relevance: Prisma.sql`ts_rank(t.search_vector, websearch_to_tsquery('english', ${q}))`,
     createdAt: Prisma.sql`t.created_at`,
     ...
     priority: Prisma.sql`CASE t.priority WHEN 'urgent' THEN 4 WHEN 'high' THEN 3 WHEN 'medium' THEN 2 WHEN 'low' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END`,
